cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "Lonely runner conjecture: dense windows and the covering framework, an obstruction catalogue, a perturbation bound, a reduction, and eight runners, with leave-one-out values of tight Goddyn-Wong vectors"
abstract: >-
  Seven manuscripts on the lonely runner conjecture (dense velocity windows, obstructions to certificate-based
  proofs, perturbations of the tight instance, a reduction to a single crux, a covering framework, eight
  runners, and leave-one-out values of tight Goddyn-Wong vectors), with the Lean 4 sources they cite, and the programs and certificates that regenerate their finite
  computations. Versions 1.1.0 and 1.1.1 extend the dense-window manuscript: the inequality for m distinct speeds in
  [1,2m] is Lean-verified for every m with 2 <= m <= 126 (including m = 94, by a tree of three pinned
  cover-capacity certificates), and the eight-runner manuscript records a Lean theorem with the standard
  axioms only, compiled outside the lake build. Version 1.2.0 adds a Markdown note with a hand proof and a
  Lean proof of a special case of a known theorem (four moving runners, phases restricted to (1/5)Z; the
  shifted conjecture for four moving runners, with arbitrary real phases, was proved with computer assistance
  by Alcantara, Criado and Santos, arXiv:2506.13379). Version 1.3.0 adds a second Markdown note with a
  computation-free proof and a Lean proof of a sub-case of the same known theorem (four moving runners,
  arbitrary real phases, distinct positive speeds with gcd 1, at least one divisible by 5; the sub-case is
  not new, and speed vectors with no multiple of 5 are not covered). Version 1.3.1 is a text-only correction
  of the prior-art statement for that note: as we read Malikiosis, Santos and Schymura (arXiv:2411.06903,
  version 2), the speed vectors with at least two speeds divisible by 5, or with speed sum (after dividing by
  the gcd) at least 196, are treated there without a computer search of their own, so the computation-free
  proof is not claimed to be new for those; no theorem or proof changed. Version 1.4.0 adds a third Markdown
  note, with written proofs that follow Lean proofs of five classes of speed vectors of the same known theorem
  (four moving runners, arbitrary real phases, four distinct positive speeds; classes defined by divisibility
  of the speeds), and a program with its outputs for a census of the classes. None of the five is a new
  theorem. Version 1.5.0 adds two further classes to that note, with Lean proofs and written proofs: exactly
  one speed divisible by 7, with the other three speeds in one of two patterns modulo 7, and exactly two
  speeds divisible by 3 with a non-resonance condition. None of the seven classes is a new theorem. Of the
  2,133,561 primitive speed vectors with speed sum at most 195, 2,002,342 lie in a class with a Lean proof in
  this archive and 131,219 lie in no class, including (1,2,3,4), (1,3,4,6) and (1,3,4,7) (version 1.4.0:
  1,867,559 and 266,002, superseded); vectors with speed sum at least 196 are not treated here, so the
  archive does not contain a proof of the shifted theorem for four moving runners for all speeds. Version
  1.6.0 adds a fourth Markdown note with a Lean proof of the shifted statement for four moving runners,
  arbitrary real phases and speed sum at most 195 (the 2,002,342 vectors in a class from the seven class
  theorems and the other 131,219 by data certificates checked by the Lean kernel: 130,914 depth-0
  certificates and 305 exact time covers; the data are not trusted, only the soundness theorems, the class
  test and the loop lemmas). That statement is the case of speed sum at most 195 of a known theorem (Alcantara,
  Criado and Santos with computer assistance, after the reduction of Malikiosis, Santos and Schymura); it is
  not a new theorem and not the full four-runner theorem, because the case of speed sum at least 196 is not
  formalised here, and no priority claim is made for the formalisation. A rebuild needs about 4.5 hours and
  must be done one module at a time (see lean/README.md). Statements carry the
  labels given in each manuscript;
  several results are finite computations that are not formalized. No proof of the lonely runner conjecture
  is claimed. The manuscripts have not been peer reviewed.
